  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 301 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nUpper level ridging remains over the Desert Southwest throughout \nFriday and moving eastward through the weekend, bringing the start \nof anomalous warm air advection to the region. 850 mb temps are 25 \nto 30C across most of western Nebraska Friday afternoon, supporting \nsurface highs in the low to mid 90s across the region. A lee trough \nis expected to develop across the northern plains during Friday \nafternoon/evening. Combine this with southerly low level flow \nbringing moisture from the Gulf should create a moist, unstable \nairmass that could bring increased thunderstorm chances, \nparticularly across the eastern half of the CWA (along and east\nof HWY 83). This also matches up well with SPC's day 3 severe \nweather outlook of isolated thunderstorms being possible. \n\nHeading into the weekend, the upper ridge starts to become flattened \ndue to an upper trough starting to move across the Pacific Northwest \nthroughout the day Saturday. This will allow for strong \nsouthwesterly flow aloft to become a driving force in way of much \nwarmer surface highs. Currently, it appears that Saturday afternoon \nwill be the peak of the mini heatwave. Most of the CWA is forecasted \nto see highs in the mid 90s with areas along and south of I 80 \npotentially reaching triple digits. Sunday will be slightly cooler \nwith highs \"only\" reaching the low to mid 90s across the area. The \nwarmer temperatures should provide ample instability for any \nembedded shortwaves to take advantage of to produce at least daily \nthunderstorm chances through Sunday, though uncertainty remains if \nany of those thunderstorms will be severe. \n\nMoving into early next week, the upper ridge deamplifies and an \nupper trough moves across western CONUS. A surface front may stall \nout across Kansas into far southeast Nebraska Monday into Tuesday to \nprovide chances for showers and thunderstorms each evening. It is \nimportant to note that while showers and thunderstorms will be \npossible, widespread rainfall is not expected with guidance \nsuggesting only a 10 - 30 percent chance of at least a 0.25\" Monday \nthrough Wednesday. Surface highs will back down to near normal over \nnorth central Nebraska (mid to upper 80s) and slightly above \nnormal across southwest Nebraska (low to mid 90s) Monday through\nWednesday.\n\nWith the stronger flow aloft and the warmer temperatures coming, \nsome fire weather concerns creep into the periphery. Focus lands on \nFriday through Sunday for at least elevated fire weather conditions. \nAnomalous high temperatures with humidity in the low to mid 20s\nwill combine with gusty westerly winds peaking on Saturday at \n25 - 30 mph to create elevated fire weather conditions along and\nwest of HWY 83.",
